EXTRA = {
"Apparels and Accessories": {
"offer": ("Define the apparel offer before you buy any stock. Choose one customer group, such as office workers in Sao Paulo, and select a narrow range of sizes, colors and price points. Compare three nearby competitors and list their prices, fabrics and return rules. Design a core line of basics plus a small seasonal line, because the city weather can change within a day. Calculate the landed cost of each item, including fabric, sewing, packaging and freight, then set the price from a target margin. Test two samples with ten buyers and record which one they pick. Revise the line before you order a full batch. Check the current official text for labeling and tax rules.",
"A seller plans a cotton shirt. Fabric costs R$18, sewing R$12 and packaging plus freight R$5, so landed cost is 18 + 12 + 5 = R$35. She wants a 50 percent margin on the selling price, so she divides by 0.50 and gets R$70. A competitor sells a similar shirt at R$75, so R$70 is realistic. If she orders 40 shirts, the stock cost is 40 x 35 = R$1,400, and selling all of them brings R$2,800."),
"people": ("Organize people and service so customers feel welcome and decide faster. Assign one person to the floor, one to stock and one to online messages, and describe each role in a single page. Train staff to ask two questions, who the clothes are for and for which occasion, then recommend three items instead of thirty. Practice measuring sizes and explaining exchange rules in plain words. Create a simple script for answering messages within ten minutes. Evaluate service by reading five customer comments each week and noting repeated complaints. Rehearse difficult cases, such as a late delivery, so staff can apologize and solve the problem calmly. Check the current official text for employment rules.",
"A small shop gets 20 message inquiries a week. Before training, staff answered slowly and only 4 became sales, a rate of 4 / 20 = 20 percent. After a script and a ten-minute reply goal, 7 of 20 bought, which is 35 percent. With an average ticket of R$120, weekly sales rose from 4 x 120 = R$480 to 7 x 120 = R$840, a gain of R$360 per week."),
"growth": ("Grow the apparel business by measuring a few numbers every week: visits, sales, average ticket and returns. Compare each product against its cost and mark the slow ones for a discount or a bundle. Plan the calendar around local dates, such as Mothers Day, winter and year-end, and order stock six weeks early. Ask buyers for a photo and a short review, and offer a modest reason to return, such as early access to new pieces. Calculate how many repeat customers you keep each quarter. Experiment with one new channel at a time and judge it by sales, not by likes. Check the current official text for marketplace and tax rules.",
"A shop sold 150 pieces in a month at an average ticket of R$90, so revenue was 150 x 90 = R$13,500. It tracked 60 customers and found 18 had bought before, so the repeat rate was 18 / 60 = 30 percent. After a loyalty message, repeat buyers rose to 24 of 60, or 40 percent. Six more repeat buyers at R$90 each add 6 x 90 = R$540 a month."),
},
"Beauty": {
"offer": ("Design the beauty offer around a clear problem your clients have, such as little time, frizzy hair or sensitive skin. List every service you can perform, then select the three you do best and sell them as named packages. Calculate the time each service takes, the products it uses and the cost of your hour. Compare local prices, then set yours so a full day still covers rent, products and your own pay. Prepare a simple menu with price, duration and what is included. Test a package with five regular clients and revise it from their feedback. Check the current official text for hygiene and licensing rules.",
"A nail studio offers a package with manicure and pedicure. Products cost R$9, the cleaning and tools cost R$6 and the service takes 100 minutes. The owner values her time at R$30 per hour, which is 30 x 100 / 60 = R$50. Total cost is 9 + 6 + 50 = R$65. She prices the package at R$110, leaving R$45 above cost. Six packages a week give 6 x 110 = R$660 in revenue."),
"people": ("Build the team and the experience together. Recruit professionals by watching a real demonstration, not only by reading a resume. Define a standard welcome, a consultation and a closing routine so every client gets the same quality. Train the team to listen first, explain each step and warn about aftercare. Teach strict cleaning of tools and record each cycle in a checklist. Schedule appointments with buffer time so no client waits. Evaluate each professional by rebooking rate and complaints, then give feedback every month. Check the current official text for health, safety and labor rules before hiring anyone.",
"A salon has an agenda of 8 slots a day per professional but clients arrive late, so only 6 are served. Adding a 10-minute buffer and a reminder message raises served slots to 7. At an average of R$80 per service and 22 working days, the extra slot adds 1 x 80 x 22 = R$1,760 a month per professional. With two professionals, the gain is 2 x 1,760 = R$3,520."),
"growth": ("Grow by keeping clients, not only finding new ones. Record each visit, service and date, then calculate how many clients return within 45 days. Send a friendly reminder when it is time for a touch-up. Ask satisfied clients to introduce one friend and thank them with a small gift of your choice. Compare services by profit per hour and promote the best ones. Photograph results with permission and publish them regularly. Review numbers monthly: new clients, returning clients, average ticket and cancellations. Adjust the plan using evidence. Check the current official text for advertising and data privacy rules.",
"A studio has 100 active clients and 55 return within 45 days, a return rate of 55 percent. After reminders the rate is 65 percent, so 10 more clients return. With an average ticket of R$90, that is 10 x 90 = R$900 more each cycle. If the studio runs about 8 cycles a year, the yearly gain is 900 x 8 = R$7,200."),
},
"Dental": {
"offer": ("Design the dental offer around what patients need most and what your clinic can deliver safely. List the procedures, group them into prevention, restoration and aesthetics, and describe each in plain words. Estimate chair time, materials and laboratory cost for every procedure. Compare local price ranges and decide where you want to stand. Prepare a written treatment plan with options, costs and stages so the patient can decide calmly. Design a prevention routine, such as a six-month check-up, to build long relationships. Check the current official text from the professional council and health authority for rules on advertising, records and infection control.",
"A clinic prices a restoration. Materials cost R$25, assistant time R$15 and the procedure takes 45 minutes of a chair that costs R$120 per hour to run, which is 120 x 0.75 = R$90. Total cost is 25 + 15 + 90 = R$130. The clinic sets the price at R$220, so the margin is 220 - 130 = R$90, or about 41 percent of the price."),
"people": ("Build a team that makes patients feel safe. Define roles for the dentist, assistant, reception and cleaning, and write each routine as a checklist. Train the reception to explain the schedule, the payment options and the preparation for each visit. Teach the whole team to speak calmly, avoid technical words and confirm that the patient understood. Practice the sterilization cycle and record each batch. Evaluate service through a short question after each visit and review the answers weekly. Support continued study for the clinical team. Check the current official text for staff qualifications, health rules and patient privacy.",
"A clinic books 30 appointments a week but 6 patients miss them, a no-show rate of 6 / 30 = 20 percent. The reception starts confirming by message one day before, and no-shows fall to 3. At R$150 per appointment, the clinic recovers 3 x 150 = R$450 a week. Over 4 weeks that is R$1,800 of agenda time that no longer sits empty."),
"growth": ("Grow the clinic by tracking the patient journey. Count new patients, accepted treatment plans, completed treatments and returns for the check-up. Compare how many plans patients accept and discuss the main reason when they decline. Create reminders for six-month visits and for unfinished treatments. Ask for reviews in an honest way and answer every comment politely. Measure the monthly revenue per chair and the cost of each new patient. Plan equipment purchases only after you calculate how many procedures pay for them. Check the current official text for advertising limits in dentistry and for patient data rules.",
"A clinic presents 40 treatment plans a month and 20 are accepted, which is 50 percent. After explaining stages and payment options better, 26 are accepted, or 65 percent. With an average plan of R$800, accepted value rises from 20 x 800 = R$16,000 to 26 x 800 = R$20,800, a gain of R$4,800 a month. Over a year, the same rate would add R$57,600 in accepted treatment value."),
},
"Cleaning Services": {
"offer": ("Design the cleaning offer by deciding who you serve and what is included. Separate residential, office and post-construction cleaning, since each needs different tools and time. List the tasks of each package and write what is not included. Measure how long a typical job takes and the cost of products, transport and labor. Compare local prices, then price by area or by visit so you protect your margin. Prepare a one-page proposal with the checklist, the price and the schedule. Visit the place before quoting. Check the current official text for labor, safety and product rules.",
"A cleaner quotes an office of 120 square meters. The job takes 2 workers for 4 hours, and labor costs R$20 per hour each, so labor is 2 x 4 x 20 = R$160. Products and transport add R$40. Total cost is R$200. She sets the price at R$300, so she earns 300 - 200 = R$100, or 33 percent of the price, per visit."),
"people": ("Build a reliable team because clients judge you by who enters their space. Define the cleaning checklist for each room and train every worker to follow it in the same order. Demonstrate the correct use of products, gloves and equipment, and explain why mixing certain products is dangerous. Assign teams and routes to reduce travel time. Create a quick report after each visit with photos and a signature. Evaluate quality by random inspections and client feedback. Reward punctuality and care. Check the current official text for employment, protective equipment and safety rules.",
"A team of 2 workers cleans 3 homes a day, taking 40 minutes of travel between visits. By grouping clients in the same neighborhood, travel falls to 20 minutes per move. The team saves 2 moves x 20 = 40 minutes a day, enough for part of a fourth home. If a fourth visit earns R$150 on 10 days a month, the gain is 10 x 150 = R$1,500."),
"growth": ("Grow by turning one-time jobs into regular contracts. Offer weekly or monthly plans with a clear price and a small advantage, such as priority scheduling. Record each client, the frequency and the date of the next visit. Calculate the monthly income from recurring clients and compare it with one-time jobs. Ask for a referral after a good visit. Measure cancellations, complaints and the time each job takes, then fix the weak points. Plan equipment purchases only after you check how many jobs pay for them. Check the current official text for contracts and tax rules.",
"A business has 10 recurring clients paying R$280 a month, so recurring income is 10 x 280 = R$2,800. It converts 5 one-time clients into recurring ones by offering a small plan advantage. The new income is 5 x 280 = R$1,400 a month, so total recurring income rises to R$4,200, an increase of 50 percent. Over twelve months, the new clients add 1,400 x 12 = R$16,800."),
},
"Warehousing and Storage Services": {
"offer": ("Design the storage offer around the cargo and the customer. Decide whether you sell pallet spaces, shelves, cold storage or short-term boxes, and describe what each includes. Measure usable space, not total space, because aisles and loading areas take room. Calculate cost per pallet position, including rent, energy, labor, insurance and equipment. Define services such as receiving, counting, picking and packing, and price each one separately. Compare nearby warehouses and state your advantage, such as location or opening hours. Write simple terms for damage and delay. Check the current official text for storage, fire and insurance rules.",
"A warehouse has 200 pallet positions and monthly costs of R$16,000. Cost per position is 16,000 / 200 = R$80. Assuming 80 percent will be occupied, the break-even cost per used position is 16,000 / 160 = R$100. The owner prices storage at R$140 per pallet. With 160 pallets occupied, revenue is 160 x 140 = R$22,400 and the gap above cost is R$6,400."),
"people": ("Organize the people so goods move safely and accurately. Define roles for receiving, storage, picking and dispatch, and write a step-by-step routine for each. Train staff in lifting, equipment operation and labeling. Teach everyone to count at receipt and note any damage on the document at once. Schedule shifts around truck arrivals. Practice the response to a customer who asks where an item is, with a system answer in under two minutes. Evaluate accuracy by monthly stock counts. Check the current official text for workplace safety, equipment licenses and labor rules.",
"A warehouse picks 500 orders a month with 2 percent errors, so 10 orders are wrong. Each error costs R$60 in redelivery and goodwill, a loss of 10 x 60 = R$600. After training and double-check labels, errors fall to 0.6 percent, or 3 orders, costing 3 x 60 = R$180. The monthly saving is 600 - 180 = R$420."),
"growth": ("Grow the warehouse by watching occupancy and turnover. Calculate the percentage of positions in use each week and the average days goods stay. Contact customers whose volume is shrinking before they leave. Offer a better price for longer commitments and extra services for busy seasons. Track revenue per square meter and compare it with last quarter. Plan expansion only when occupancy stays high for several months. Collect feedback on delays and errors, and publish a monthly service report to customers. Check the current official text for contracts and tax rules.",
"A warehouse with 200 positions averaged 120 occupied last quarter, which is 60 percent. A campaign to local shops fills 30 more positions, giving 150, or 75 percent. At R$140 per position, monthly revenue rises from 120 x 140 = R$16,800 to 150 x 140 = R$21,000. The difference is R$4,200 a month, while fixed costs stay unchanged, so most of it becomes margin."),
},
"Transportation and Courier Services": {
"offer": ("Design the delivery offer around one clear promise, such as same-day delivery inside a set area. Map the zones you serve and the usual travel time between them. Calculate the cost per kilometer, including fuel, maintenance, driver time, tolls and parking. Define vehicle types, package sizes and weight limits. Set prices by zone and package size, and add a fee for urgent delivery. Describe pickup windows and proof of delivery. Compare competitors and state what makes you more reliable. Check the current official text for vehicle, cargo and insurance rules.",
"A courier drives 80 kilometers on a typical day. Fuel and maintenance cost R$0.90 per kilometer, so vehicle cost is 80 x 0.90 = R$72. Driver time for 8 hours at R$15 is R$120. Daily cost is R$192. If he makes 24 deliveries, cost per delivery is 192 / 24 = R$8. He charges R$14, earning R$6 per delivery above cost."),
"people": ("Build a team of drivers and dispatchers who protect the customer promise. Define routines for loading, checking the address, confirming delivery and reporting problems. Train drivers to handle packages gently, communicate delays politely and follow safe driving rules. Teach dispatch to group deliveries by area so trips are shorter. Create a simple message template for the customer when the driver is nearby. Evaluate each driver by on-time rate, damage reports and feedback. Rest and breaks matter for safety. Check the current official text for driver licensing, working hours and labor rules.",
"A dispatcher assigns 24 deliveries to a driver by order of arrival, covering 80 kilometers. After grouping by neighborhood, the same 24 deliveries need 62 kilometers. At R$0.90 per kilometer, the saving is (80 - 62) x 0.90 = R$16.20 a day. Over 22 working days, that is 16.20 x 22 = R$356.40 per driver per month, and with 3 drivers about R$1,069."),
"growth": ("Grow the courier business by tracking reliability and repeat customers. Measure the on-time rate, damaged packages, cost per delivery and number of deliveries per customer. Visit small shops and offer a simple weekly route with a fixed price. Ask customers what delays hurt them most and fix that first. Compare profit by zone and drop or reprice the zones that lose money. Plan vehicle purchases only when a steady volume covers the monthly cost. Review results every month and share them with the team. Check the current official text for contracts and tax rules.",
"A courier serves 6 shops that each send 20 packages a week at R$12, so weekly revenue is 6 x 20 x 12 = R$1,440. Adding 2 shops with the same volume adds 2 x 20 x 12 = R$480. If cost per package is R$8, the profit of the new shops is 2 x 20 x (12 - 8) = R$160 a week, or R$640 over 4 weeks."),
},
"3D Printing Business": {
"offer": ("Design the 3D printing offer by choosing a niche, such as spare parts, prototypes, gifts or architectural models. Select the materials and printer settings you can deliver with steady quality. Print test pieces and measure time, filament used and failures. Calculate cost per piece, including material, electricity, machine wear, finishing and your hour. Define a minimum order and a delivery time. Prepare a simple price table by size and complexity, and a way to receive files. Compare local prices and online listings. Check the current official text for product safety and intellectual property rules.",
"A printed phone stand uses 60 grams of filament at R$100 per kilogram, so material is 0.06 x 100 = R$6. It prints in 4 hours, and electricity plus machine wear are R$1.50 per hour, or R$6. Finishing takes 10 minutes worth R$4. Cost is 6 + 6 + 4 = R$16. At a price of R$40, the margin is R$24 per piece."),
"people": ("Organize the work so quality does not depend on one person. Write a checklist for receiving a file, checking it, choosing settings, printing, inspecting and packing. Train a second person to run the printers and to recognize common failures, such as warping and weak layers. Define a clear conversation with the customer: ask about use, strength and color before you quote. Explain limits honestly. Schedule long prints overnight and finishing in the day. Evaluate quality by comparing the finished piece with the drawing. Check the current official text for workshop safety and labor rules.",
"A workshop has 5 printers and a 15 percent failure rate on 100 jobs, so 15 jobs are reprinted. Each reprint wastes R$12 of material and time, a loss of 15 x 12 = R$180. After a checklist and bed preparation routine, failures fall to 6 percent, or 6 jobs, costing 6 x 12 = R$72. The monthly saving is 180 - 72 = R$108."),
"growth": ("Grow by turning your best jobs into repeatable products. Review sales and identify the three items that sell most and earn most per hour. Create small batches and list them with clear photos. Ask business customers, such as designers and repair shops, for recurring orders. Track machine hours used, revenue per hour and failure rate. Add a printer only when current machines are busy most of the week. Test new materials on small orders first. Publish examples with permission. Check the current official text for designs you may copy and for tax rules.",
"One printer works 200 hours a month and earns R$2,400, so revenue per hour is 2,400 / 200 = R$12. The owner redirects hours from low jobs at R$8 per hour to a standard product earning R$16 per hour. If 80 hours move, the gain is 80 x (16 - 8) = R$640 a month, raising revenue from R$2,400 to R$3,040."),
},
"App Development": {
"offer": ("Design the app offer by defining one user, one problem and one result. Interview five possible users and write their current way of solving the problem. List the smallest set of features that delivers the result, and postpone everything else. Sketch the main screens on paper and test them with users before you write code. Estimate development hours, tests and maintenance. Decide how the app earns money, such as a subscription or a service contract. Compare similar apps and state your difference. Check the current official text for data protection and store rules.",
"A developer estimates a first version at 160 hours and values her time at R$60 per hour, so build cost is 160 x 60 = R$9,600. Hosting and tools cost R$150 a month. If a business client pays R$1,200 a month for the app, the monthly margin is 1,200 - 150 = R$1,050. The build cost is recovered in 9,600 / 1,050, about 9.1 months."),
"people": ("Organize the team and the user support around clear responsibilities. Define who decides priorities, who writes code, who tests and who answers users. Hold a short weekly meeting to review progress and blockers. Write acceptance criteria for every feature in plain words so everyone knows when work is done. Train the support person to reproduce a problem and report it with steps and screenshots. Use code review so knowledge is shared. Evaluate the team by delivered features and user problems solved, not by hours. Check the current official text for contractor, employment and data rules.",
"A team of 3 delivers 9 features in a month but 3 return with bugs. Adding test steps and code review reduces returns to 1. If each returned feature costs 6 hours at R$60, rework falls from 3 x 6 x 60 = R$1,080 to 1 x 6 x 60 = R$360. The monthly saving is R$720, and the team gets that time back for new work."),
"growth": ("Grow the app by measuring how people actually use it. Track new users, active users, the step where they leave and how many return after a week. Read support messages and group them by theme. Release small updates often and compare results before and after each. Test pricing with a small group before you change it for everyone. Ask happy users for reviews. Calculate the cost to win a user and the revenue each user brings. Stop work on features nobody uses. Check the current official text for data protection and subscription rules.",
"An app gets 500 downloads a month and 100 users stay active after a week, a retention of 20 percent. A better first screen lifts retention to 28 percent, or 140 users. If 10 percent of active users pay R$20 a month, paying users rise from 10 to 14. Monthly revenue grows from 10 x 20 = R$200 to 14 x 20 = R$280, a gain of R$80 for each month of downloads."),
},
"Small and Medium Entrepreneur Business": {
"offer": ("Design the offer by starting from your customer, not from your product. Describe the customer, the problem and the moment they feel it. List what you sell, select the one or two items with the best margin and the clearest demand, and make them easy to understand. Calculate full cost, including your own pay, taxes you must set aside and idle time. Compare prices around you, then decide where you want to be. Test the offer with ten real customers before you expand. Write the promise in one sentence. Check the current official text for registration, tax and consumer rules.",
"An owner sells a service with direct cost of R$60. Monthly fixed costs are R$3,000 and she expects 100 sales. Fixed cost per sale is 3,000 / 100 = R$30, so full cost is 60 + 30 = R$90. She wants a 30 percent margin on price, so she divides 90 by 0.70 and gets about R$129. She rounds the price to R$129."),
"people": ("Organize people and service by writing down how things are done. Describe three main routines, such as selling, delivering and handling complaints, in simple steps. Define who is responsible for each and who replaces them when absent. Train every person, including family helpers, to greet customers and solve small problems alone. Set a simple rule for refunds and delays. Evaluate service by asking each customer one question after the sale. Meet the team weekly for fifteen minutes. Reward useful ideas. Check the current official text for hiring, working hours and contract rules.",
"A small shop receives 8 complaints a month, each taking 30 minutes of the owner's time, or 4 hours. After writing a simple rule for exchanges and training the team, the staff solves 6 alone. The owner now spends 2 x 0.5 = 1 hour. At a value of R$40 per hour, the owner saves 3 x 40 = R$120 a month and customers wait less."),
"growth": ("Grow by watching your numbers every week, not only at year end. Record sales, costs, cash balance and number of customers. Calculate profit and the cash needed for the next thirty days. Choose one growth action at a time, such as a new customer group or a repeat-purchase offer, and measure its result. Keep a reserve for slow months. Review prices twice a year against your costs. Ask customers why they chose you and why others left. Plan hiring and equipment from cash, not from hope. Check the current official text for taxes, credit and reporting rules.",
"A business sells R$20,000 a month with total costs of R$17,000, so profit is R$3,000 or 15 percent of sales. A repeat-purchase offer lifts sales by 10 percent, to R$22,000, while variable cost rises by R$1,200 and fixed costs stay. New profit is 22,000 - 18,200 = R$3,800. The gain is R$800 a month, and margin is about 17 percent."),
},
"Outsourcing Services": {
"offer": ("Design the outsourcing offer by selecting the tasks your client wants to stop doing, such as bookkeeping support, customer service or data entry. Define the scope, the hours, the quality standard and what the client must send. Write the deliverables and deadlines in simple language. Calculate cost per hour or per task, including training, tools, management time and idle capacity. Compare the price of hiring in-house and show the client the difference honestly. Offer a short pilot with a clear measure of success. Check the current official text for contract, labor and data protection rules.",
"A client spends R$3,500 a month on an in-house employee for order entry. The outsourcing provider handles 1,200 orders at R$2.20 each, so the price is 1,200 x 2.20 = R$2,640. The client saves 3,500 - 2,640 = R$860 a month. The provider's cost is R$1.60 per order, or R$1,920, so its margin is 2,640 - 1,920 = R$720 a month, about 27 percent of the price."),
"people": ("Organize the people who deliver the service so quality does not change when a person is absent. Document each process with steps, examples and common mistakes. Train at least two people for every client task. Define a single contact for each client and a clear way to report problems. Hold a short weekly review with the client to confirm priorities. Evaluate work by accuracy, deadlines and client comments. Protect client information by limiting access to those who need it. Check the current official text for labor, subcontracting and confidentiality rules.",
"A provider processes 1,000 tasks a month with 5 percent errors, so 50 are corrected. Each correction takes 15 minutes at R$20 per hour, costing 50 x 0.25 x 20 = R$250. After a checklist and a second reviewer, errors drop to 2 percent, or 20 tasks, costing 20 x 0.25 x 20 = R$100. The monthly saving is R$150, and client trust improves."),
"growth": ("Grow by deepening current clients before you chase new ones. Report each month what you delivered, how fast and at what quality, using simple numbers. Ask the client which related task is causing trouble and propose a small pilot. Track revenue per client, margin per client and how long clients stay. Review prices each year against your real cost. Record the reasons when a client leaves. Plan hiring only after demand is steady for several months. Share a short case result, with permission and without names. Check the current official text for contracts and renewal rules.",
"A provider has 5 clients paying R$2,500 a month, so revenue is 5 x 2,500 = R$12,500. Two clients accept an added task for R$600 each, adding R$1,200. Revenue becomes R$13,700, a rise of about 9.6 percent. If the added work needs 20 extra hours costing R$18 each, cost is R$360, leaving R$840 more margin each month, or R$10,080 over a year if both clients stay."),
},
"Electronics Sales & Repair Services": {
"offer": ("Design the offer by separating what you sell from what you repair. Choose the devices you will serve, such as phones and notebooks, and list the common faults. Estimate parts cost, time and risk for each repair. Set a diagnosis step and decide whether it is free or priced. Define a warranty period in days and write what it covers. Select a small catalog of accessories with fast turnover. Compare local prices for the same parts and repair. Prepare a receipt that records the device, the fault and the agreed price. Check the current official text for warranty, consumer and waste disposal rules.",
"A phone screen repair uses a part that costs R$150, and the technician spends 45 minutes valued at R$40 per hour, which is R$30. Tools and packaging add R$5. Cost is 150 + 30 + 5 = R$185. The shop charges R$290 with a 90 day warranty. Margin is 290 - 185 = R$105 per repair, about 36 percent of the price."),
"people": ("Organize the team so customers trust you with personal devices. Define roles for reception, repair and sales. Train reception to record the fault, accessories received and the condition of the device, and to explain the time and the price in plain words. Teach technicians to follow a checklist, label parts and test every repair before delivery. Call the customer when the repair is ready and when a new problem appears. Evaluate the work by returns within the warranty and by customer comments. Protect customer data. Check the current official text for data protection and workshop rules.",
"A shop completes 80 repairs a month and 8 come back under warranty, a return rate of 10 percent. Each return costs R$45 in time and parts. Loss is 8 x 45 = R$360. After a final test checklist, returns fall to 3, costing 3 x 45 = R$135. The shop saves R$225 a month and keeps the customers who would have complained."),
"growth": ("Grow by combining repairs, sales and follow-up. Record each customer, device and date, then contact them after six months to check the device and offer protection accessories. Track repairs per week, average ticket, return rate and parts waiting time. Keep a small stock of the most used parts and avoid buying parts that rarely sell. Compare suppliers on price and failure rate. Ask satisfied customers for a review and a referral. Measure which accessories earn the most per shelf space. Check the current official text for warranty, imports and tax rules.",
"A shop sells 50 accessories a month at R$35 with cost of R$20, earning 50 x 15 = R$750. It places a case and a charger with each repair and, after follow-up messages, sells 20 more a month. The extra margin is 20 x 15 = R$300. Monthly accessory margin rises from R$750 to R$1,050, an increase of 40 percent."),
},
}
